<article class="env-proposition" id="01GK">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01GK">Proposition <span data-tag="01GK">3.1.3.13</span>.</a> Suppose we are given a commutative diagram of simplicial sets </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ A \ar [r]^-{f} \ar [d]^{i} &amp;  X \ar [d]^{q} \\ B \ar [r]^-{g} \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur]^{ \overline{f} } &amp;  S, } \]
</div>
<p> where $i$ is a monomorphism and $q$ is a Kan fibration. Then the simplicial set $\operatorname{Fun}_{A/ \,  /S}(B, X)$ is a Kan complex. If $i$ is anodyne, then the Kan complex $\operatorname{Fun}_{A/ \,  /S}(B,X)$ is contractible. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      By virtue of Remark <a data-tag="01GJ" href="/tag/01GJ">3.1.3.11</a>, the simplicial set $\operatorname{Fun}_{A/ \,  /S}( B, X)$ can be identified with a fiber of the restriction map 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \theta : \operatorname{Fun}(B,X) \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}(A,X) \times _{ \operatorname{Fun}(A,S) } \operatorname{Fun}(B,X). \]
</div>
<p>
       Theorem <a data-tag="00TK" href="/tag/00TK">3.1.3.1</a> guarantees that $\theta $ is a Kan fibration, so its fibers are Kan complexes by virtue of Remark <a data-tag="00T6" href="/tag/00T6">3.1.1.9</a>. If $i$ is anodyne, then $\theta $ is a trivial Kan fibration (Theorem <a data-tag="014E" href="/tag/014E">3.1.3.5</a>), so its fibers are contractible Kan complexes (Remark <a data-tag="0074" href="/tag/0074">1.5.5.10</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
